+### [Adaptive Density Control](9188-adaptive-density-control.md)
+Adaptive Density Control (ADC) is a dynamic optimization strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that adjusts the number and spatial distribution of Gaussian primitives during training to prevent under-reconstruction (missing geometry) and over-reconstruction (coarse approximations). It solves the problem of static initialization by using view-space positional gradient magnitudes, screen size, and opacity to trigger cloning, splitting, and pruning operations at regular intervals, ensuring the representation remains compact while capturing fine details. It sets itself apart from static initialization methods by dynamically balancing addition and removal during optimization rather than statically removing primitives, which avoids suboptimal simplification outcomes caused by inefficient spatial distribution.
 
-### Adaptive Density Control [9188-adaptive-density-control.md]
-Adaptive Density Control (ADC) is a dynamic optimization strategy used in 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) training to adjust the number and spatial distribution of Gaussian primitives [S1][S5]. It solves the problem of under-reconstruction (missing geometry) and over-reconstruction (coarse approximations) by periodically adding, cloning, or removing Gaussians based on their contribution to the rendering error [S1][S5]. It sets itself apart from static pruning methods (e.g., LightGaussian) by balancing addition and removal during optimization to maintain efficient spatial distribution, avoiding suboptimal simplification outcomes [S1].
+### [Pixel-GS](9189-pixel-gs.md)
+Pixel-GS is a densification strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that modifies the gradient-based splitting criteria to account for the spatial footprint of each Gaussian primitive. It scales the positional gradient by the number of pixels a Gaussian influences, ensuring that primitives causing error across many pixels are prioritized for refinement. Additionally, it applies a distance-dependent scaling factor to the gradient to suppress the creation of "floaters" in background regions. This approach yields state-of-the-art rendering quality compared to standard 3DGS, though it typically results in a higher number of Gaussian primitives.
 
