+### Parallel Respawning and Densification with Edge Case Handling
+#### definition
+- [S6, unofficial] A densification strategy where respawn and densify operations are performed in parallel, requiring specific edge case handling to prevent hierarchy degeneration.
+#### step
+- [S6, unofficial] If two sibling nodes both need to be respawned, only respawn the right node. This causes the left node to become a new dead leaf, which will be respawned in the next densification iteration.
+- [S6, unofficial] If a node needs to be respawned whose sibling is not a leaf node, replace the entire parent node with the subtree of the node being respawned.
+- [S6, unofficial] Apply a pruning strategy: zero the opacity of Gaussians that have not contributed for a number of iterations equal to twice the size of the training set. These will be respawned in the next densification iteration.
 
 ## Background
-The concept of adaptive density control originated with the foundational 3D Gaussian Splatting paper by Kerbl et al. (2023), which introduced the heuristic of splitting Gaussians based on 2D gradient magnitude and screen size [Parent]. Subsequent research has expanded this into a diverse family of strategies, including score-based methods [S1], MCMC-based approaches [7852-3dgs-mcmc.md], and geometry-constrained densification [7448-training-objective-and-densification.md]. The field has also seen significant development in pruning and opacity management to mitigate floaters and reduce memory usage [9314-pruning-and-opacity-management.md].
+The concept of adaptive densification originated with the introduction of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [Parent]. The original method used a simple heuristic based on 2D positional gradient magnitudes and screen-space size to split or clone Gaussians, and prune those with low opacity. Subsequent research has expanded this family to address specific limitations such as memory efficiency [S1][S2, unofficial], dynamic scene modeling [S1], sparse-view reconstruction [S1], and computational cost [S3, S4, S5].
 
